WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.302 --> 00:00:02.055
The Flying Hotel In the evening of May 6, 1937, a massive luxury airship called the

2
00:00:02.056 --> 00:00:11.038
Hindenburg had nearly completed its trip to America from Europe. Longer than ten blue whales, it was

3
00:00:11.039 --> 00:00:15.702
the biggest object ever to fly. With its lounges, private rooms, and gourmet food, the Hindenburg

4
00:00:15.703 --> 00:00:25.117
was like a gigantic flying hotel. And that night it was filled with passengers. Passing thunderstorms had

5
00:00:25.118 --> 00:00:33.434
made landing difficult, so the Hindenburg spent hours floating over the Atlantic coast, waiting out the

6
00:00:33.435 --> 00:00:40.013
storms. When the ship was finally ready to land in a New Jersey field, the crew dropped

7
00:00:40.014 --> 00:00:50.674
down ropes for the men on the ground. It was a routine procedure that had been

8
00:00:50.675 --> 00:00:57.175
done many times before. Inside the airship passengers eagerly pressed their faces to the windows, scanning the

9
00:00:57.176 --> 00:01:06.660
crowd below. Suddenly the people on the ground gasped in shock and horror; flames were shooting

10
00:01:06.661 --> 00:01:11.323
out from the rear of the ship! A newsman reporting from the site cried, "It's burst into

11
00:01:11.324 --> 00:01:17.655
flames! It's burst into flames, and it's falling, it's crashing! . . . Oh, the humanity!

12
00:01:17.656 --> 00:01:22.915
. . . All the passengers screaming . . . This is the worst thing I've ever

13
00:01:22.916 --> 00:01:24.804
witnessed." Werner Franz, the 14-year-old cabin boy, was saved from burning when a ruptured tank soaked

14
00:01:24.805 --> 00:01:28.718
him with water. One passenger, a trained acrobat, hung from a windowsill until he could jump

15
00:01:28.719 --> 00:01:33.313
to safety. Others were not as lucky. Within half a minute, the mighty Hindenburg lay burning on

16
00:01:33.314 --> 00:01:35.123
the ground. Thirty-six people, many of them crew members, had lost their lives. An Accident or

17
00:01:35.124 --> 00:01:40.465
Not? Until the day the Hindenburg exploded, passenger airships had had perfect safety records. Also called zeppelins

18
00:01:40.466 --> 00:01:48.792
after their inventor, Count Ferdinand von Zeppelin, German airships like the Hindenburg had rigid bodies with

19
00:01:48.793 --> 00:01:54.695
a hollow interior. The huge, balloon-like ships were filled with hydrogen, the lightest gas, so that they

20
00:01:54.696 --> 00:01:59.954
could float. Immediately after the disaster, experts began investigating what caused the explosion. To this day

21
00:01:59.955 --> 00:02:03.454
people are unsure of what really happened. Because the Hindenburg was a German ship, and the German

22
00:02:03.455 --> 00:02:11.609
Nazi party was gaining power in Europe, some people suspected that a bomb was planted. But

23
00:02:11.610 --> 00:02:19.848
most people believed the fire was an accident, set off by a spark of static electricity that

24
00:02:19.849 --> 00:02:28.088
ignited hydrogen leaking from the ship. In more recent years, experts have speculated that the explosion

25
00:02:28.089 --> 00:02:29.929
was caused by a highly flammable coating on the airship's outer skin. The Age of Zeppelins Hugo

26
00:02:29.930 --> 00:02:36.414
Eckener, a talented zeppelin pilot, eventually took over the Zeppelin Company started by Count von Zeppelin.

27
00:02:36.415 --> 00:02:43.564
Eckener had long dreamed of connecting Europe and the Americas through flight. He finally fulfilled this dream

28
00:02:43.565 --> 00:02:49.317
through the zeppelin. At first the great airships had been used during wartime to locate enemy

29
00:02:49.318 --> 00:02:55.331
ships and carry bombs. But Eckener soon realized that they could also be used to carry

30
00:02:55.332 --> 00:03:02.219
passengers across the Atlantic Ocean. At the time boats were the only way people could journey between

31
00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:05.986
Europe and the Americas—a long and often difficult voyage. The zeppelin could offer a smoother and

32
00:03:05.987 --> 00:03:15.074
more scenic way to travel. In 1929 a passenger airship called the Graf Zeppelin circled the globe,

33
00:03:15.075 --> 00:03:24.869
flying from Germany to Japan, and arriving in California. Its success encouraged the Zeppelin Company to

34
00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:30.019
begin making plans for a second airship—a bigger and better one with more safety equipment—called the Hindenburg

35
00:03:30.020 --> 00:03:40.609
The Hindenburg became the first zeppelin to fly regularly between Europe and the Americas. In 1936,

36
00:03:40.610 --> 00:03:48.274
its first year of operation, it crossed the Atlantic Ocean thirty-four times, carrying thousands of passengers and

37
00:03:48.275 --> 00:03:54.282
traveling two hundred thousand miles. While a boat could take over a week to cross the

38
00:03:54.283 --> 00:03:57.909
Atlantic Ocean, the Hindenburg could do it in about half the time and much more comfortably. Europe

39
00:03:57.910 --> 00:04:03.244
and America became wild with excitement about the flying airship—the world's first real airline. Children on

40
00:04:03.245 --> 00:04:06.260
both sides of the Atlantic played zeppelin games. Household objects such as pocketknives and needle packets came

41
00:04:06.261 --> 00:04:09.664
with pictures of zeppelins on them. Countries around the world printed zeppelin stamps. Being onboard the

42
00:04:09.665 --> 00:04:14.836
Hindenburg was a special experience. The passengers had their own rooms and enjoyed breathtaking views and

43
00:04:14.837 --> 00:04:20.763
luxury treatment. Mealtimes featured gourmet food served on expensive plates that people often kept as souvenirs. A

44
00:04:20.764 --> 00:04:26.339
very light piano was even specially designed for the ship so passengers could enjoy music. At

45
00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:32.402
night people could leave their shoes in the hallway to be shined, just as they would in

46
00:04:32.403 --> 00:04:39.989
a fine hotel. There was even a smoking room with airtight doors and a single lighter

47
00:04:39.990 --> 00:04:45.814
chained to the wall, to prevent any possibility of a fire. The airships were so popular that

48
00:04:45.815 --> 00:04:47.465
soon an American company was making plans to build them too. The End of an Era

49
00:04:47.466 --> 00:04:52.956
The Hindenburg was the greatest airship of all, but it was also the one that changed everything.

50
00:04:52.957 --> 00:04:59.138
Its disastrous explosion in 1937 abruptly brought an end to the age of zeppelins. For the

51
00:04:59.139 --> 00:05:05.048
first time in history, photographers were able to capture an enormous tragedy as it happened. By the

52
00:05:05.049 --> 00:05:11.620
next day, horrifying pictures of the explosion were shown all over the world in movie theaters

53
00:05:11.621 --> 00:05:17.545
and newspapers. No one could ever look at a zeppelin again without picturing the Hindenburg bursting into

54
00:05:17.546 --> 00:05:20.855
flames. All future transatlantic zeppelin flights were canceled. Within a few years, growing safety concerns and

55
00:05:20.856 --> 00:05:28.276
the dawning of airplanes caused the great airships to disappear from the sky. As the zeppelin commander

56
00:05:28.277 --> 00:05:35.786
Eckener had once said, the airship was like "a fabulous silvery fish . . . floating

57
00:05:35.787 --> 00:05:40.267
quietly in the ocean of air . . . It seemed to be coming from another

58
00:05:40.268 --> 00:05:42.108
world and to be returning there like a dream." A Memorable Adventure In 1939, just two years

59
00:05:42.109 --> 00:05:52.508
after the Hindenburg disaster, a passenger airplane flew across the Atlantic for the first time. A

60
00:05:52.509 --> 00:05:58.007
new era of air travel had begun! But the great flying airships—which were at the forefront of

61
00:05:58.008 --> 00:06:06.175
air travel—were never forgotten. Even after the disaster, many survivors said their experience onboard the Hindenburg

62
00:06:06.176 --> 00:06:14.230
was so memorable that they would definitely ride a zeppelin again. And today, in both California and

63
00:06:14.231 --> 00:06:18.814
Germany, smaller, helium-filled zeppelins have recently appeared in the skies, carrying sightseers. Werner Franz lived to

64
00:06:18.815 --> 00:06:25.815
be the last survivor of the 1937 tragedy. As a young cabin boy, he had dreamed of

65
00:06:25.816 --> 00:06:32.481
becoming a zeppelin captain—but that dream vanished along with the airship itself. Many years later, when

66
00:06:32.482 --> 00:06:37.081
asked about his most unforgettable zeppelin adventure, Werner spoke of a trip to South America. The Hindenburg

67
00:06:37.082 --> 00:06:42.809
had met the Graf Zeppelin in the middle of its flight over the Atlantic. The massive

68
00:06:42.810 --> 00:06:46.538
ships slowly circled one another in silent greeting, like giant whales in the air. It was a

69
00:06:46.539 --> 00:06:50.888
sight that delighted both the crew and passengers. "That moment," Werner said, "was just for us."

